  "marked": false,
  "answer": "<p>The correct solution is to enable <b>Amazon Macie</b>. Macie uses ML and pattern matching to scan S3 buckets for sensitive data such as PII, financial information, or credentials, and can automatically alert administrators when risks are detected. It is specifically designed for identifying data security and privacy risks across S3.</p><p>The other options are not suitable for this scenario: <i>'Use AWS GuardDuty, which can monitor all data in S3 and provide security insights specific to the information stored.'</i> is not accurate because while GuardDuty can monitor S3 data events and detect suspicious activity, it cannot actually scan the contents of objects within S3 for sensitive information. <i>'Set up AWS Inspector for S3 to leverage machine learning for detecting security risks and generate CloudWatch notifications.'</i> is incorrect because Inspector is not designed for S3 data or content scanning; it's intended for EC2 instances and container image assessments. <i>'Deploy Amazon Athena with S3, create Athena tables, and use SQL queries to search for security issues in the data.'</i> is not a scalable or automated solution for sensitive data detection and would require extensive manual setup, custom queries, and maintenance, making it impractical.</p><p><b>Tip:</b> For scanning S3 buckets to identify exposure of PII and other forms of sensitive data, Amazon Macie is the go-to managed service. Know the scope and capabilities of Macie versus GuardDuty, as they are frequently tested in AWS certification exams.</p>",
  "query": "<p>Your company stores large volumes of data in Amazon S3, and multiple apps frequently access (read/write) these buckets. An auditor is concerned about potential exposure of sensitive information—such as files containing customers' personally identifiable information (PII)—within these buckets. The auditor wants an efficient way to rapidly identify possible security concerns related to the contents of S3. What is the optimal AWS-native approach to address this requirement?</p>",
